4. use the Toolkit in paid consulting or other professional services;
5. use the Toolkit internally for commercial or noncommercial purposes;
6. include the Toolkit in a product or service when the Toolkit is not the primary commercial value of that product or service; and
7. permit other persons to exercise these rights on the Recipient's behalf.

The Owner intends this grant to let people pick up the Toolkit, improve it, share it, and use it in real work.

## 3. Permitted Commercial Use

A Recipient may, without a separate commercial license:

1. charge for time, expertise, analysis, implementation, support, training, or consulting that uses the Toolkit;
2. use the Toolkit within an organization;
3. include the Toolkit as a non-primary component of a broader commercial product or service; and
4. distribute the Toolkit or a Derivative Work without charge.

A use remains permitted when the Recipient earns money from work performed with the Toolkit. The restriction in section 4 applies when the Toolkit itself is the primary product or service sold.

## 4. Toolkit Offering restriction

A Recipient must obtain a separate written commercial license from the Owner before the Recipient offers a Toolkit Offering, including when the Recipient:

1. sells copies of the Toolkit or a Derivative Work as the primary product;
2. charges a fee primarily for access to or use of the Toolkit or a Derivative Work;
3. offers the Toolkit or a Derivative Work as the primary function of a paid hosted service, software-as-a-service product, managed service, or commercial agent service; or
4. sublicenses the Toolkit or a Derivative Work for a fee when the Toolkit itself is the primary subject of the sublicense.

Incidental use does not create a Toolkit Offering. The Owner will consider the offering's stated purpose, principal functionality, customer reason for purchase, and source of commercial value.

